TASK: Expanding target ring + drive feel polish (Phase 3)

ACCEPTANCE
- A tap blooms a ring within a frame: at the resolved destination for a drive,
  and at the car for a dead-zone honk, so no touch is silent.
- Pulse: radius 0.12 -> 0.55 world units over 0.45s, ease-out growth (most of
  the expansion lands in the first quarter, so the tap reads instantly),
  linear fade, hidden when finished. Drawn at y = 0.03, above the +0.02
  asphalt, so it never z-fights with the road it marks.
- One ring, not a pool: the newest tap restarts the pulse, so a mash cannot
  litter the street with stale rings. The mesh is a unit ring that is scaled,
  so a pulse allocates nothing after construction.
- Drive feel verified by watching it drive: the car turns in place to its
  heading, then holds a constant 1.6 u/s on the line, including through both
  junctions and both bends, and a mid-route tap replaces the route.

TEST-FIRST
- ringFrame (pure) is pinned: starts small and opaque, ends at full radius and
  zero opacity, monotonic growth and fade, fastest expansion first, and both
  negative and over-long elapsed times clamp.

VERIFICATION METHOD (worth recording)
- A 0.45s pulse is easy to miss between tool round-trips, so the ring was
  verified in two steps: first slowed to a 4s pulse (with the pulse timing
  sampled live over 6 seconds to confirm the curve), then restored and
  re-verified at the real speed by holding a tap loop open so a young, opaque
  ring was guaranteed on screen. Temporary changes (the slowed constant and a
  debug hook) were removed before committing; `grep -n TEMP` is clean.
- One genuine visual finding: a tap outside the town is clamped to the map
  edge, so its ring sits half over the edge of the world. The marker is
  accurate — that IS the destination the car drives to — but it reads as
  "go where there is no ground". Left as-is; noted for the polish pass.

